1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,040
This video is about L L one grammars. These are the grammars that a recursive descent parser can use.

2
00:00:07,490 --> 00:00:26,221
We learn two simple tests that show a grammar is not L L one: common prefixes and left recursion. We learn how to refactor the grammar to remove both problems, and how to convert E B N F to B N F. Then we solve three exercises from the course notes.

3
00:00:26,671 --> 00:00:40,772
The course notes study three classes of grammars. The largest is the context free grammars. A general parser for them takes order n cubed time, and needs a nondeterministic pushdown automaton.

4
00:00:41,222 --> 00:00:53,489
Inside it is L L one. We can write parsers for these grammars by hand, with recursive descent. They parse in linear time, with a deterministic pushdown automaton.

5
00:00:53,939 --> 00:01:00,445
Inside L L one are the regular languages, which we recognize with finite automata.

6
00:01:00,895 --> 00:01:07,850
The question for this video and the next one is: is a given grammar inside L L one, or outside?

7
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:15,255
L L one is short for three things. The first L: the parser reads the input from left to right.

8
00:01:15,705 --> 00:01:23,107
The second L: it builds a leftmost derivation. It always expands the leftmost nonterminal first.

9
00:01:23,557 --> 00:01:29,488
And 1: it looks at one token of lookahead. That is the next token, not yet consumed.

10
00:01:29,938 --> 00:01:32,861
Here the parser has read i d and the arrow.

11
00:01:33,311 --> 00:01:37,129
It now expects a Factor. Factor has four alternatives.

12
00:01:37,579 --> 00:01:46,475
The lookahead is the not sign. Only the first alternative, not Factor, can start with it. So the parser picks it, with no guessing.

13
00:01:46,925 --> 00:01:51,640
That is the L L one property. One token predicts the alternative.

14
00:01:52,090 --> 00:02:00,751
A recursive descent parser has one function per nonterminal. Here is a sketch of the function for Factor. It is not lab code.

15
00:02:01,201 --> 00:02:08,007
The first test looks at the next token. If it is the not sign, the parser takes the first alternative.

16
00:02:08,457 --> 00:02:12,894
If it is an open parenthesis, it takes the second alternative.

17
00:02:13,344 --> 00:02:16,160
If it is an identifier, it calls Var.

18
00:02:16,610 --> 00:02:18,765
Otherwise it must be a constant.

19
00:02:19,215 --> 00:02:31,012
Each test looks at one token, and the tests must not overlap. When one token can start two alternatives, the parser cannot choose. Then the grammar is not L L one.

20
00:02:31,462 --> 00:02:43,387
The notes list three things that put a grammar outside L L one. The first is common prefixes: two alternatives of the same nonterminal that start with the same symbols.

21
00:02:43,837 --> 00:02:49,107
The second is left recursion: a production for A whose right side starts with A.

22
00:02:49,557 --> 00:02:55,359
The third is ambiguity: a string with two parse trees. Video 18 covered it.

23
00:02:55,809 --> 00:03:04,385
The two simple tests can only show that a grammar is not L L one. After we refactor, the new grammar might be L L one.

24
00:03:04,835 --> 00:03:10,979
To know for sure, we need the full test with the predict sets. That is video 19 b.

25
00:03:11,429 --> 00:03:18,491
Here is the example from the notes. A statement is an assignment: i d, colon equals, expression.

26
00:03:18,941 --> 00:03:23,869
Or a call: i d, open parenthesis, expression, close parenthesis.

27
00:03:24,319 --> 00:03:28,052
The parser expects a statement. The lookahead is i d.

28
00:03:28,502 --> 00:03:34,731
Both alternatives start with i d. One token of lookahead cannot choose between them.

29
00:03:35,181 --> 00:03:41,645
The tokens that decide are colon equals or the open parenthesis. They are the second token.

30
00:03:42,095 --> 00:03:47,322
So one token of lookahead is not enough. This grammar is not L L one.

31
00:03:47,772 --> 00:03:57,457
The fix is called left factoring. Here is the general pattern from the notes. Goal has two alternatives, and both start with the same Prefix.

32
00:03:57,907 --> 00:04:03,070
We keep one copy of the Prefix, and follow it with a new nonterminal, Tail.

33
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:06,848
Tail has the two suffixes as its alternatives.

34
00:04:07,298 --> 00:04:18,882
The new grammar accepts the same strings. The parser reads the shared prefix first. It chooses between the suffixes only after the prefix, when the next token is different.

35
00:04:19,332 --> 00:04:25,775
Apply the pattern. The prefix is i d. The new grammar is: statement goes to i d Tail.

36
00:04:26,225 --> 00:04:33,137
Tail goes to colon equals expression, or to open parenthesis, expression, close parenthesis.

37
00:04:33,587 --> 00:04:40,819
Parse i d, colon equals, x. Statement has only one alternative. The parser reads i d.

38
00:04:41,269 --> 00:04:47,583
Now it expects a Tail. The lookahead is colon equals, so it picks the assignment alternative.

39
00:04:48,033 --> 00:04:56,929
The two Tail alternatives start with different tokens, colon equals and open parenthesis. Their predict sets do not overlap.

40
00:04:57,379 --> 00:05:02,286
A check in python: both grammars give the same strings up to length 7.

41
00:05:02,736 --> 00:05:11,611
Now the second problem. A list of identifiers, separated by commas. List goes to List comma i d, or to i d.

42
00:05:12,061 --> 00:05:16,669
The first alternative starts with List itself. That is left recursion.

43
00:05:17,119 --> 00:05:22,708
Write the function for List from the first alternative. Its first action is to call List.

44
00:05:23,158 --> 00:05:30,219
That call does not read a token. So the new call sees the same lookahead, i d, and calls List again.

45
00:05:30,669 --> 00:05:35,661
The call stack grows, and no input is consumed. The parser never stops.

46
00:05:36,111 --> 00:05:44,687
Both alternatives also start with i d, so the predict sets overlap. A left recursive grammar is not L L one.

47
00:05:45,137 --> 00:05:50,172
Here is the pattern from the notes. Goal goes to Goal Suffix, or to Prefix.

48
00:05:50,622 --> 00:06:01,801
What strings does Goal make? Each step adds a Suffix on the right. The last step must use the Prefix alternative. So the string is a Prefix, then the Suffixes.

49
00:06:02,251 --> 00:06:06,709
So the solution starts with Prefix, then a new nonterminal, Tail.

50
00:06:07,159 --> 00:06:12,578
Tail goes to Suffix Tail, or to epsilon. Epsilon stops the repetition.

51
00:06:13,028 --> 00:06:17,060
Both grammars give a Prefix, then zero or more Suffixes.

52
00:06:17,510 --> 00:06:23,441
The new Tail is right recursive, and the parser reads a token before each recursive call.

53
00:06:23,891 --> 00:06:29,565
Apply the pattern to List. The Prefix is i d, and the Suffix is comma i d.

54
00:06:30,015 --> 00:06:36,138
So List goes to i d Tail, and Tail goes to comma i d Tail, or to epsilon.

55
00:06:36,588 --> 00:06:44,908
Derive i d comma i d comma i d. We start from List. The orange box is the leftmost nonterminal.

56
00:06:45,358 --> 00:06:48,323
List has one alternative: i d Tail.

57
00:06:48,773 --> 00:06:52,763
The lookahead is a comma, so Tail takes comma i d Tail.

58
00:06:53,213 --> 00:06:56,925
Another comma. Tail takes comma i d Tail again.

59
00:06:57,375 --> 00:07:00,980
Now the input is finished. Tail takes epsilon.

60
00:07:01,430 --> 00:07:12,907
Each choice used one token of lookahead. A python check found no overlap in the predict sets, and the parser agrees with the language on all strings up to length 7.

61
00:07:13,357 --> 00:07:21,720
Compare the parse trees for i d comma i d comma i d. The old grammar builds a tree that leans to the left.

62
00:07:22,170 --> 00:07:27,909
The new grammar builds a tree that leans to the right. Each Tail holds the rest of the list.

63
00:07:28,359 --> 00:07:34,801
So the two grammars accept the same strings, but they build different trees. They are weakly equivalent.

64
00:07:35,251 --> 00:07:44,019
This matters for operators. Video 18 showed that a left associative operator, such as minus, needs left recursion.

65
00:07:44,469 --> 00:07:53,557
If we remove the left recursion, we also change the associativity. The notes say there is no simple solution in B N F.

66
00:07:54,007 --> 00:08:04,034
The notes suggest E B N F with repetition. The parser reads the repetition with a loop, and it can build a left associative tree from it.

67
00:08:04,484 --> 00:08:13,423
This is the E B N F grammar for the F language from the notes, Figure 4.5. It uses two extra notations.

68
00:08:13,873 --> 00:08:20,699
The star means repetition: zero or more. It appears three times, in Program, Expr and Term.

69
00:08:21,149 --> 00:08:25,352
The bar means alternation. It appears in Factor and Constant.

70
00:08:25,802 --> 00:08:34,037
B N F has neither the star nor the bar. We convert to B N F so that we can compute the sets for the full test.

71
00:08:34,487 --> 00:08:41,569
Take Expr. It is a Term, then plus Term, repeated zero or more times. The starred group is marked.

72
00:08:42,019 --> 00:08:46,329
Replace the starred group with a new nonterminal. Call it TermTail.

73
00:08:46,779 --> 00:08:51,216
TermTail goes to plus Term TermTail. That is one more repetition.

74
00:08:51,666 --> 00:08:55,762
Or TermTail goes to epsilon. That stops the repetition.

75
00:08:56,212 --> 00:09:01,097
So Expr still makes a Term, followed by zero or more plus Term groups.

76
00:09:01,547 --> 00:09:07,329
The bar is easy. Write each alternative as its own production with the same left side.

77
00:09:07,779 --> 00:09:16,589
Here is the full B N F grammar, Figure 4.6 in the notes. It has 17 productions and no star or bar.

78
00:09:17,039 --> 00:09:22,949
Three new nonterminals come from the three stars: FList, TermTail and FactorTail.

79
00:09:23,399 --> 00:09:26,300
Each of them has an epsilon production.

80
00:09:26,750 --> 00:09:41,961
We checked the conversion in python. A recognizer written from the E B N F and the B N F grammar agree on every token string up to length 6. Video 19 b uses this grammar for the full test.

81
00:09:42,411 --> 00:09:56,896
Now an exercise from the notes. This grammar is not suitable for a top down predictive parser. Fix the problems, then make the L L one parsing table. The terminals are the letters a, b and c.

82
00:09:57,346 --> 00:09:58,989
Pause the video and try it.

83
00:09:59,439 --> 00:10:07,737
Look for the two simple problems. R goes to R b c. The right side starts with R, so this is left recursion.

84
00:10:08,187 --> 00:10:14,737
Q goes to b b c, and Q goes to b c. Both start with b. That is a common prefix.

85
00:10:15,187 --> 00:10:18,472
The two L productions start with R and with Q.

86
00:10:18,922 --> 00:10:20,842
R starts with a or c.

87
00:10:21,292 --> 00:10:27,777
Q starts with b. The sets a, c and b do not overlap, so the L productions are fine.

88
00:10:28,227 --> 00:10:36,675
Fix the left recursion first. R has two non recursive alternatives, a b a and c a b a. Each is a Prefix.

89
00:10:37,125 --> 00:10:40,816
The Suffix is b c, from R goes to R b c.

90
00:10:41,266 --> 00:10:45,426
So R goes to a b a R 1, or to c a b a R 1.

91
00:10:45,876 --> 00:10:51,529
R 1 goes to b c R 1, or to epsilon. The notes call it R prime.

92
00:10:51,979 --> 00:10:55,414
Now left factor Q. The common prefix is b.

93
00:10:55,864 --> 00:11:00,259
Q goes to b Qtail. Qtail goes to b c, or to c.

94
00:11:00,709 --> 00:11:02,778
The L productions do not change.

95
00:11:03,228 --> 00:11:09,756
A python check confirms that the new grammar gives the same strings as the old one, up to length 10.

96
00:11:10,206 --> 00:11:18,441
The table has a row for each nonterminal and a column for each lookahead token. A cell holds the production to use.

97
00:11:18,891 --> 00:11:24,629
For L: R starts with a or c, so the a and c columns take L goes to R a.

98
00:11:25,079 --> 00:11:29,623
Q starts with b, so the b column takes L goes to Q b a.

99
00:11:30,073 --> 00:11:37,092
For R: lookahead a takes the a b a alternative. Lookahead c takes the c a b a alternative.

100
00:11:37,542 --> 00:11:41,126
For R 1: lookahead b takes b c R 1.

101
00:11:41,576 --> 00:11:58,024
When does R 1 go to epsilon? When the next token can follow R. In L goes to R a, an a follows R. So the a column takes R 1 goes to epsilon. Video 19 b explains this rule, the follow set.

102
00:11:58,474 --> 00:12:01,311
Q has one production, for lookahead b.

103
00:12:01,761 --> 00:12:04,641
Qtail: b takes b c, and c takes c.

104
00:12:05,091 --> 00:12:09,614
No cell holds two productions. So the new grammar is L L one.

105
00:12:10,064 --> 00:12:20,325
This table is our solution; the notes give only the grammar. A table driven parser built from it agrees with the language on every string up to length 10.

106
00:12:20,775 --> 00:12:30,674
Next exercise. Refactor this grammar to L L one form. S is an identifier with a list in parentheses, or in square brackets.

107
00:12:31,124 --> 00:12:34,495
A is a list of identifiers separated by commas.

108
00:12:34,945 --> 00:12:36,587
Pause the video and try it.

109
00:12:37,037 --> 00:12:42,029
There are two problems. The S alternatives have the common prefix i d.

110
00:12:42,479 --> 00:12:47,087
And A is left recursive: A goes to A comma i d starts with A.

111
00:12:47,537 --> 00:12:50,801
Left factor S. S goes to i d T.

112
00:12:51,251 --> 00:12:57,246
T goes to open parenthesis A close parenthesis, or to open bracket A close bracket.

113
00:12:57,696 --> 00:13:03,669
Remove the left recursion from A, with the same pattern as List. A goes to i d U.

114
00:13:04,119 --> 00:13:07,405
U goes to comma i d U, or to epsilon.

115
00:13:07,855 --> 00:13:13,444
A python check: same strings up to length 9, and no overlapping predict sets.

116
00:13:13,894 --> 00:13:24,347
Last exercise. The start symbol is expr, and i d is any single letter. Explain why an L L one parser cannot parse this grammar.

117
00:13:24,797 --> 00:13:26,440
Pause the video and try it.

118
00:13:26,890 --> 00:13:31,327
The problem is in expr. The first production starts with i d.

119
00:13:31,777 --> 00:13:36,215
Now follow the second production. expr goes to term term tail.

120
00:13:36,665 --> 00:13:38,841
term goes to factor factor tail.

121
00:13:39,291 --> 00:13:44,880
factor can go to i d. So the second production can also start with i d.

122
00:13:45,330 --> 00:13:53,351
Both expr productions can start with i d. It is a common prefix, but it is hidden behind three nonterminals.

123
00:13:53,801 --> 00:14:03,487
With lookahead i d, the parser cannot choose. A python check of the predict sets finds exactly this one overlap, on i d.

124
00:14:03,937 --> 00:14:14,091
To summarize. L L one means left to right, leftmost derivation, one token of lookahead. One token must predict the alternative.

125
00:14:14,541 --> 00:14:22,904
A common prefix blocks the choice. Left factor it: keep the prefix, and move the suffixes into a new Tail nonterminal.

126
00:14:23,354 --> 00:14:35,365
Left recursion makes recursive descent loop. Rewrite Goal as Prefix Tail, with Tail going to Suffix Tail or epsilon. The strings stay the same, but the trees change.

127
00:14:35,815 --> 00:14:47,015
To convert E B N F to B N F, split each bar into productions, and replace each star with a new nonterminal that uses recursion and epsilon.

128
00:14:47,465 --> 00:15:02,569
Look for prefixes that hide behind nonterminals. And remember: the simple tests can only show that a grammar is not L L one. The next video gives the full test: nullable, first, follow and predict sets.
